---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import SourcedBlock from "../../../components/SourcedBlock.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import StoryState from "../../../components/StoryState.astro";
import StateEvent from "../../../components/StateEvent.astro";
import BarChart from "../../../components/story/BarChart.astro";
import DataTable from "../../../components/story/DataTable.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import OutletCheck from "../../../components/story/OutletCheck.astro";
import CheckedBlock from "../../../components/story/CheckedBlock.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import { readPublishedState, eventPath } from "../../../lib/state.mjs";
import {
  event, talarico, paxton, computed, filing, magaCash, poll, ieGeneral,
  marchNotice, grahamNotice, tracker, delRay, tcm, monthlyPayees, outsideMoney, sb2, timeline, millions, usd,
  propertyVotes, pollEnsemble, magaDonors,
} from "../../../data/texassenate202609.mjs";
const state = readPublishedState().events.get("event-texas-senate-september-2026");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-texas-senate-september-2026");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);
const moneyRows = [
  ["Raised", millions(talarico.receipts), millions(paxton.receipts)],
  ["Spent", millions(talarico.disbursements), millions(paxton.disbursements)],
  ["Cash on hand", millions(talarico.cashOnHand), millions(paxton.cashOnHand)],
  ["Share from small gifts", `${computed.talaricoUnitemizedPct} percent`, `${computed.paxtonUnitemizedPct} percent`],
  ["Ads booked so far (AdImpact, via the Tribune)", "$40.1 million", "$0.3 million"],
];
---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/texas-senate/">The Texas Senate race</a><span>September 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative">MAGA INC. &middot; September 5, 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </details></nav>
      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>On Saturday, September 5, MAGA Inc., the super PAC that backs President Trump, filed a 48-hour notice for two independent expenditures totaling $10 million to Del Ray Media LLC: $5 million against James Talarico, the Democrat, and $5 million for Ken Paxton, the Republican. Schedule E lists a disbursement or obligation date of September 4.<Cite s="fec-form24-cover" passage="Date Signed: 09/05/2026" /><Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="Date of Disbursement or Obligation = 09/04/2026" /></li>
          <li>The money is not a gift to Paxton's campaign. It is independent spending: the PAC buys the ads itself and certified under penalty of perjury that it did not coordinate with any candidate. Four outlets called it a donation.<Cite s="fec-form24-certification" passage="not made in cooperation, consultation, or concert" /><Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="donating $10 million" /></li>
          <li>The ads say Talarico "voted to make it easier to increase property taxes." He did vote no, twice, on the 2019 state law that makes cities and counties get voter approval before raising property tax revenue more than 3.5 percent. Both spots also put source slates on screen: the tax spot cites Punchbowl News, the Tax Foundation, Thomson Reuters, the Texas House Journal, and the Texas Tribune; the health spot cites Talarico for Texas, Navigant, and the Hoover Institution, and puts "$4,000,000,000,000" on screen from Punchbowl and a June 6, 2025 Tax Foundation page.<Cite s="magainc-ad-paxton-2026-09-05" passage="James Tallarico voted to make it easier to increase property taxes, supported a $4 trillion tax hike" /><Cite s="magainc-ad-talarico-frame-024" passage="TALARICO" /><Cite s="magainc-ad-paxton-frame-007" passage="$4,000,000,000,000" /></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>

      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on MAGA INC.'s September 5 48-hour notice (FEC file {filing.fileNumber}), the two 30-second spots saved from the MAGA War Room posts, checked by ear with local speech recognition and frame by frame on September 8, Paxton's July 31 Texas Promise video, MAGA Inc.'s 2025-26 Form 3X receipts, the committee's August monthly report covering July, the Federal Election Commission's candidate pages for the July quarterlies, the Commission's 2026 independent-expenditure bulk file saved September 8, MAGA INC.'s March 11 notice for a Georgia special election, Texas House Journal roll calls on property-tax bills in 2019, 2023, and 2025, both campaigns' issue pages saved September 8, the Texas Secretary of State's 2026 dates page, November 3 election-law calendar, independent-candidate list, and Candidate Bio Portal capture for the November general, the University of Texas / Texas Politics Project August poll and the TPPF/Overton August poll, MAGA Inc. and Ken Paxton's posts on X, and coverage checked against those records. The $10 million figure is the FEC filing. It is not an ad-tracking firm's estimate.</p>
      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The filing, then the ads</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 5, MAGA Inc.'s treasurer, Charles Gantt, signed a 48-hour notice to the Federal Election Commission. A 48-hour notice is the form a political committee files within two days of paying for, or committing to, spending like this in the run-up to an election. This one lists two lines of $5 million each to Del Ray Media LLC of Alexandria, Virginia, purpose "PLACED MEDIA: CONNECTED TV & DIGITAL ADVERTISING": one to oppose Talarico, one to support Paxton. Schedule E dates the disbursement or obligation September 4 and the public distribution September 5.<Cite s="fec-form24-cover" passage="Date Signed: 09/05/2026" /><Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="PLACED MEDIA: CONNECTED TV & DIGITAL ADVERTISING" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">An independent expenditure is money spent on a message that names a candidate and asks voters to elect or defeat that person, without giving the money to the campaign. MAGA Inc. is a hybrid political committee with a non-contribution account: one account limited by contribution caps and one unlimited account used for independent spending. The $10 million here is that kind of spending, not a check to Paxton's campaign committee. On the same notice Gantt certified, "under penalty of perjury," that the spending was "not made in cooperation, consultation, or concert with, or at the request or suggestion of, any candidate or authorized committee."<Cite s="fec-maga-inc-committee" passage="Hybrid PAC (with Non-Contribution Account) - Nonqualified" /><Cite s="fec-form24-certification" passage="not made in cooperation, consultation, or concert" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Both spots are about taxes, and both end with the same line: "MAGA Inc. is responsible for the content of this advertising." The anti-Talarico spot says he "supported a trillion dollar tax hike, higher property taxes, and higher taxes on social security." The pro-Paxton spot says he "voted to make it easier to increase property taxes, supported a $4 trillion tax hike," and "supports a radical health care plan that could close rural hospitals." Both promise a Paxton plan: doubling the child tax credit, write-offs for medical expenses, and a $50,000 deduction for first-time homes. The words of both are below, and each claim is checked against the record further down.<Cite s="magainc-ad-talarico-2026-09-05" passage="James Talo Rico supported a trillion dollar tax hike, higher property taxes, and higher taxes on social security." /><Cite s="magainc-ad-paxton-2026-09-05" passage="a radical health care plan that could close rural hospitals and cost thousands in new taxes" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Alex Pfeiffer, a spokesman for MAGA Inc., wrote the same day: "High Tax Talarico wants to take money out of Texans' pockets." Paxton posted that Talarico "has a record of raising taxes on working families" and thanked Trump for his support. JT Ennis, a Talarico campaign spokesperson, said in a statement carried by the Associated Press that "Billionaire-backed dark money groups are lying about James Talarico" and that Talarico "was proud to vote for the largest property tax cut in Texas history." The Texas Tribune added that Talarico's "economic plan includes raising taxes on corporations and billionaires and expanding tax credits for parents and lower-income workers."<Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="a spokesman for MAGA" /><Cite s="paxton-x-2026-09-05" passage="Protecting the Texas Promise Agenda" /><Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Billionaire-backed dark money groups are lying about James Talarico" /><Cite s="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="Talarico’s economic plan includes raising taxes on corporations and billionaires" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The week, and what comes after</strong><span>From the notice, the FEC filing calendar, and the Texas Secretary of State's dates.</span></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} />
          <p class="chart-source">Sixty days separate the commitment date and the election.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="Total Expenditures This Period = $10000000" /><Cite s="fec-2026-quarterly-filers" passage="10/15/2026" /><Cite s="sos-dates-2026" passage="First day of early voting by personal appearance for November 3 general election." /></p>
        </figure>

        <QuoteCard when="September 5, 2026" where="30-second spot posted by MAGA War Room, captioned “James Talarico means higher taxes for Texas”" label="Saved from the post and transcribed on September 8; the recognizer heard the surname as it is spelled here.">
          <p>Tired of paying more in taxes? Ken Paxton pushing his new plan to make living more affordable for Texans. Paxton's plan, doubling the child tax credit, big write-offs for medical expenses, and a fifty thousand dollar deduction for first-time homes. James Talo Rico supported a trillion dollar tax hike, higher property taxes, and higher taxes on social security. Tallerico, higher taxes, Paxton, lower taxes. A clear choice.. MAGA Inc is responsible for the content of this advertising.<Cite s="magainc-ad-talarico-2026-09-05" passage="doubling the child tax credit, big write-offs for medical expenses, and a fifty thousand dollar deduction for first-time homes" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <QuoteCard when="September 5, 2026" where="30-second spot posted by MAGA War Room, captioned “When it comes to taxes, Ken Paxton is the better choice”" label="Saved from the post and transcribed on September 8.">
          <p>James Tallarico voted to make it easier to increase property taxes, supported a $4 trillion tax hike, and Talarico supports a radical health care plan that could close rural hospitals and cost thousands in new taxes. Ken Paxton supports tax cuts and has a plan to let you deduct medical expenses and the down payment on your home. When it comes to taxes, Ken Paxton's the better choice. MAGA Inc. is responsible for the content of this advertising.<Cite s="magainc-ad-paxton-2026-09-05" passage="Ken Paxton supports tax cuts and has a plan to let you deduct medical expenses and the down payment on your home" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <QuoteCard when="July 31, 2026" where="Paxton campaign video, YouTube" label="Saved audio, transcribed on September 8. One contiguous span.">
          <p>That's why I'm unveiling my protecting Texas Promise Agenda. It establishes a landmark $25,000 tax deduction for all out-of-pocket medical expenses, including insurance premiums. It creates a historic $50,000 home down payment deduction to make it easier to buy a home. It institutes a $5,000 healthy living tax deduction to help Texans stay active, eat healthier, and invest in their well-being.<Cite s="paxton-texas-promise-video-2026-08" passage="That's why I'm unveiling my protecting Texas Promise Agenda" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A later line of the same video, not contiguous with that card, says "It doubles the child tax credit and makes the Trump accounts permanent."<Cite s="paxton-texas-promise-video-2026-08" passage="It doubles the child tax credit and makes the Trump accounts permanent." /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-form24-2010907" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The filing itself: MAGA INC., committee {filing.committeeId}, file {filing.fileNumber}, signed by Gantt on September 5, 2026. Schedule E, the list of independent expenditures, carries two lines of {millions(filing.opposeTalarico)} each to Del Ray Media LLC, purpose "PLACED MEDIA: CONNECTED TV & DIGITAL ADVERTISING," disbursement or obligation date September 4, public distribution September 5, election the 2026 general. One line opposes James Talarico, candidate {filing.talaricoId}; the other supports Warren Kenneth Paxton Jr., candidate {filing.paxtonId}. The notice totals {millions(filing.total)} for the period.<Cite s="fec-form24-cover" passage="Date Signed: 09/05/2026" /><Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="Total Expenditures This Period = $10000000" /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">Not a campaign gift.</span>
              CBS wrote that Trump was "donating $10 million." The Hill wrote that MAGA Inc. "gave $10 million to the Texas attorney general's Senate campaign." USA Today wrote that it "has donated $10 million to the campaign." The FEC notice is independent spending paid to an ad firm, and Gantt certified it was "not made in cooperation, consultation, or concert with" any candidate. Why it matters: Paxton's campaign never receives this money. The certification is the filer's statement that the spending was not coordinated, not proof of what happened in the room.<Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="donating $10 million" /><Cite s="thehill-hinojosa-texas-senate" passage="gave $10 million to the Texas attorney general" /><Cite s="usatoday-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="has donated $10 million to the campaign" /><Cite s="fec-form24-certification" passage="not made in cooperation, consultation, or concert" /><Cite s="fec-independent-expenditures-help" passage="made in cooperation, consultation or concert with, or at the request or suggestion of, a candidate" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">Two spots, two tax-hike numbers.</span>
              One MAGA Inc. spot says Talarico "supported a $4 trillion tax hike"; the other, paid for on the same notice, says "a trillion dollar tax hike." On screen the health spot writes "$4,000,000,000,000" and cites Punchbowl News of September 9, 2025 and a Tax Foundation page of June 6, 2025; the tax spot cites Punchbowl, a Tax Foundation page of July 4, 2025, Thomson Reuters of November 3, 2025, the Texas House Journal of May 25, 2019, and the Texas Tribune of June 12, 2019. Why it matters: the number a viewer hears depends on which spot ran, and the documents those slates name are national revenue estimates and a 2019 Texas vote, not a Talarico bill with either figure.<Cite s="magainc-ad-paxton-2026-09-05" passage="James Tallarico voted to make it easier to increase property taxes, supported a $4 trillion tax hike" /><Cite s="magainc-ad-talarico-2026-09-05" passage="James Talo Rico supported a trillion dollar tax hike, higher property taxes, and higher taxes on social security." /><Cite s="magainc-ad-paxton-frame-007" passage="$4,000,000,000,000" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Left out</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">Paxton's later property-tax yes votes.</span>
              The ads' property-tax charge is a description of two 2019 no votes on SB 2. Talarico voted yes on SB 2 in 2023, the bill Governor Abbott called "the largest property tax cut in Texas history," and yes on SB 4 in 2025. Why it matters: a viewer who hears only the 2019 votes hears a selective account of the record.<Cite s="tribune-2019-house-vote-sb2" passage="James Talarico --> N" /><Cite s="house-journal-882-day06-2023" passage="SBi2 was passed by (Record 31): 133 Yeas, 4 Nays" /><Cite s="abbott-property-tax-cut-2023" passage="the largest property tax cut in Texas history" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Left out</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">The 42-39 trial ballot.</span>
              CBS cited the University of Texas August poll for Paxton's 61 percent approval among Republicans and did not report the same poll's head-to-head: Talarico {poll.talarico} percent, Paxton {poll.paxton} percent, a {computed.pollGapPoints}-point gap inside that poll's {poll.moeWeighted}-point weighted margin. Why it matters: the number CBS used says Paxton is popular with his party; the number it left out is a race with no leader inside the margin.<Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="61% approval rate among Republicans" /><Cite s="ut-txpp-poll-2026-08" passage="42% of Texas voters preferring Democrat James Talarico and 39% planning to vote for Republican Ken Paxton" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Who pays</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">MAGA Inc.'s donors, not the campaigns.</span>
              The $10 million comes out of the {millions(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)} MAGA Inc. held on July 31, {computed.shareOfMagaCashPct} percent of it. Neither campaign's account moves. Paxton's committees held {millions(paxton.cashOnHand)} at the end of June; Talarico's held {millions(talarico.cashOnHand)}.<Cite s="fec-maga-inc-august-monthly" passage="403450026.85" /><Cite s="fec-paxton-july-quarterly" passage="$1,755,783.35" /><Cite s="fec-talarico-july-quarterly" passage="$68,555,930.42" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="catch-minor">Smaller slips, same outlet: CBS named the vendor "Del Ray Media Inc." (the notice says LLC) and wrote that MAGA Inc. "donated $827,000 to Sen. Darline Graham's campaign" in South Carolina; that August 24 notice is independent spending on text messages and phone calls, the same category error as the Texas line.<Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Del Ray Media Inc" /><Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="DEL RAY MEDIA LLC" /><Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="donated $827,000" /><Cite s="fec-form24-2009583" passage="Calendar YTD Per Election for Office Sought = $827711.11" /></p>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The race and the money already on the books</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Ken Paxton is the Texas attorney general. "Trump’s endorsement helped Paxton to defeat the incumbent, Sen. John Cornyn, in a May primary runoff election," the Associated Press wrote; that runoff drew {millions(tcm.primaryAndRunoff)} in outside spending against Paxton from Texans for a Conservative Majority. James Talarico is "a 37-year-old state representative" and the Democratic nominee. Libertarian Ted Brown is also on the ballot.<Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="37-year-old state representative" /><Cite s="fec-ie-2026-bulk" passage="TEXANS FOR A CONSERVATIVE MAJORITY" /><Cite s="sos-goelect-candidates" passage="JAMES TALARICO" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Outside money in the general election, by committee</strong><span>Millions of dollars reported to the FEC. Bars above zero help Paxton or oppose Talarico; bars below zero help Talarico or oppose Paxton.</span></figcaption>
          <BarChart bars={outsideMoney.bars} unit="m" ariaLabel="Independent expenditures in the Texas Senate general election by committee, in millions of dollars" highlightLast={false} />
          <p class="chart-source">MAGA Inc.'s $10 million is the largest single filing. The other five committees had already reported {millions(outsideMoney.generalBeforeNotice)}.<Cite s="fec-ie-2026-bulk" passage="LONE STAR LIBERTY PAC" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">FEC independent-expenditure bulk file, saved September 8, 2026 ({ieGeneral.fileRows.toLocaleString("en-US")} rows). Later filings will add to these totals.</p>
          <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>the rows behind the bars</summary>
            <p>General-election rows (ele_type G) for candidates {filing.talaricoId} and {filing.paxtonId}, keeping the highest file number per committee, candidate, and transaction id; {ieGeneral.texasRowsRaw} Texas Senate rows became {ieGeneral.texasRowsDedup}. Lone Star Liberty PAC: ${usd(ieGeneral.loneStarLibertyOpposeTalarico)} opposing Talarico plus ${usd(ieGeneral.loneStarLibertySupportPaxton)} supporting Paxton. Truth and Courage PAC: ${usd(ieGeneral.truthAndCourageOpposeTalarico)} opposing Talarico. America PAC: ${usd(ieGeneral.americaPacOpposeTalarico)} opposing Talarico plus ${usd(ieGeneral.americaPacSupportPaxton)} supporting Paxton. Protecting America's Future: ${usd(ieGeneral.protectingAmericasFutureOpposeTalarico)} opposing Talarico. Lone Star Rising PAC: ${usd(ieGeneral.loneStarRisingOpposePaxton)} opposing Paxton plus ${usd(ieGeneral.loneStarRisingSupportTalarico)} supporting Talarico. Sum of the five committees other than MAGA Inc.: ${usd(outsideMoney.generalBeforeNotice)}.</p>
          </details>
        </figure>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Ten million dollars is a large ad buy and a small share of the buyer. MAGA Inc. reported {millions(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)} in cash on July 31, so this is {computed.shareOfMagaCashPct} percent of it. Senate Majority Leader John Thune "estimated advertising costs $8 million a week" in Texas, which makes $10 million about {outsideMoney.weeksAtThuneRate} weeks of ads. Trump told reporters the day before the filing, "I think I have close to a billion dollars in the Super PAC"; the FEC record for MAGA Inc. itself shows the {millions(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)}, and the rest, if it exists, sits in nonprofits that do not report to the FEC.<Cite s="fec-maga-inc-august-monthly" passage="403450026.85" /><Cite s="nbc-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="impeached by the Republican-led state House" /><Cite s="wh-pool-2026-09-04" passage="We already did. I think I have close to a billion dollars in the Super PAC." /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The campaigns' own money, through June 30</strong><span>From each candidate's FEC summary page and the Tribune's ad-tracking figures.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["", "Talarico", "Paxton"]} rows={moneyRows} highlightColumn={0} />
          <p class="chart-source">The two FEC summary totals, over different windows, put Talarico at {computed.receiptsRatio} dollars raised for every one of Paxton's. The Tribune's July 20 tracker, both campaigns from the start of 2025, is {tracker.talaricoSince2025.toFixed(1)} million versus {tracker.paxtonSince2025} million, {computed.trackerRatio} to 1. Neither figure is the campaigns' overall fundraising ratio. Campaigns also pay less than outside groups for broadcast ads, "meaning each dollar Talarico spends on those mediums buys more ad time than a GOP PAC," the Tribune wrote; MAGA Inc.'s buy is on connected TV and digital, "where campaigns and PACs are equal players."<Cite s="fec-talarico-july-quarterly" passage="$47,007,775.34" /><Cite s="fec-paxton-july-quarterly" passage="$7,492,915.18" /><Cite s="tribune-tracker-2026-07-20" passage="raising a cumulative $72.0 million compared to $16.8 million" /><Cite s="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="each dollar Talarico spends on those mediums buys more ad time than a GOP PAC" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Talarico's FEC page covers September 8, 2025, to June 30, 2026; Paxton's covers April 1, 2025, to June 30, 2026. The two windows differ. Unitemized individual contributions are gifts the committee did not have to list by donor name on the report. Ad figures are AdImpact's counts as quoted by the Texas Tribune on September 5.</p>
        </figure>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The Associated Press called the filing Trump's "first major midterm money move." The Texas Tribune wrote that the $10 million "is MAGA Inc.'s first expenditure of the general election." That is MAGA Inc.'s first general-election filing, not the first outside money in this race. Five other committees had already reported {millions(outsideMoney.generalBeforeNotice)} in general-election independent expenditures before the notice, led by Lone Star Liberty PAC.<Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="premier general election contest" /><Cite s="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="MAGA Inc.’s first expenditure of the general election" /><Cite s="fec-ie-2026-bulk" passage="LONE STAR LIBERTY PAC" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Two August polls, and a tracker</strong><span>Head-to-head figures as published. Margins of error are the poll's own.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={pollEnsemble.columns} rows={pollEnsemble.rows} highlightColumn={3} />
          <p class="chart-source">The University of Texas / Texas Politics Project poll of {poll.n.toLocaleString("en-US")} registered voters, August 5 to 13, has Talarico at {poll.talarico} percent and Paxton at {poll.paxton} percent; its unweighted margin is {poll.moeSample} points and its weighted margin is {poll.moeWeighted}. The TPPF/Overton poll of 1,167 likely voters, August 24 to 26, has Talarico at 44.0 percent and Paxton at 43.4, margin 2.9; with leaners, 50.0 percent each. The Texas Politics Project tracker, last updated August 13 in the pin we hold, runs from Talarico plus 4 to Paxton plus 2. Within those margins, no leader.<Cite s="ut-txpp-poll-2026-08" passage="The poll was conducted August 5-13, 2026, among 1200 registered voters" /><Cite s="tppf-overton-poll-2026-08" passage="1,167 likely Texas voters" /><Cite s="tppf-overton-toplines-2026-08" passage="James Talarico, the Democrat                                             44.0%" /><Cite s="txpp-senate-poll-tracker-2026-09-08" passage="Talarico +4" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">UT/Texas Politics Project: registered voters, YouGov, matched to 1,200. TPPF/Overton: likely voters, text-to-web, plus or minus 2.9 percent. Tracker table in the saved page lists UT, Emerson, Bush School/ReconMR, TSU/YouGov, Fox, NYT/Siena, and Quantus.</p>
        </figure>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The same UT poll asked how Texans rate Paxton's job as attorney general: {computed.paxtonApproveStatewidePct} percent approve and {computed.paxtonDisapproveStatewidePct} percent disapprove statewide, while {computed.gopApprovePaxtonPct} percent of Republicans approve, the figure CBS reported. NBC News wrote that Paxton "was impeached by the Republican-led state House in 2023 on allegations of bribery and abuse of public trust, though he was later acquitted by the state Senate after a trial," and quoted Thune saying Texas is "not one that we can lose." USA Today wrote that Cook Political Report and Fox rate the seat a toss-up. Put together: a nominee his own party rates well and the state as a whole does not, inside the margin in every poll we hold, in a seat both parties treat as winnable.<Cite s="ut-txpp-poll-crosstabs" passage="How would you rate the job Ken Paxton is doing as" /><Cite s="nbc-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="impeached by the Republican-led state House" /><Cite s="nbc-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="not one that we can lose" /><Cite s="usatoday-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="a toss-up" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">What Trump has said about the money is larger than what the FEC can see. The White House pool for September 4 recorded him saying, when asked when the super PAC would spend on the midterms, "We already did. I think I have close to a billion dollars in the Super PAC," and the Associated Press quoted him the same day: "I'm going to spend whatever amount of money necessary to try and help us," and "This is my money." The Tribune wrote that he "told reporters Friday that he had close to $1 billion under his control between various PACs and nonprofits, and that he expected to spend $400 million to $500 million on the midterms"; CBS quoted him on August 21 saying "about $850 million" and named "Securing American Greatness, a nonprofit with ties to Mr. Trump's super PAC." A nonprofit does not report its cash to the FEC, so the billion can be checked only against the committee's own {millions(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)}.<Cite s="wh-pool-2026-09-04" passage="We already did. I think I have close to a billion dollars in the Super PAC." /><Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="This is my money" /><Cite s="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="close to $1 billion under his control" /><Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="about $850 million" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The Tribune's other figures come from AdImpact, an ad-tracking firm, and they do not match the FEC file. The Tribune wrote that Lone Star Liberty PAC had spent "$18.9 million on the general election thus far" and America PAC "$1.4 million against Talarico"; the FEC general-election rows for Lone Star Liberty add to {millions(ieGeneral.loneStarLibertyOpposeTalarico + ieGeneral.loneStarLibertySupportPaxton)}. AdImpact counts ads as booked; the FEC file holds what committees have reported. We cannot reconcile the two from the records we hold. The Tribune also wrote that "Democrats have spent $41.7 million, nearly all from Talarico, to Republicans' $24.3 million," that Senate Leadership Fund "has not put any money into Texas in the general election," that Sen. John Kennedy said he was "hoping that the president's going to spend $100 or $200 million in Texas," and that Sen. Ted Cruz estimated the race could reach $200 million.<Cite s="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="$1.4 million against Talarico" /><Cite s="fec-ie-2026-bulk" passage="LONE STAR LIBERTY PAC" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="sos-goelect-candidates" kind="official" detail>
          <p>The ballot, from the Texas Secretary of State's candidate portal for the November 2026 general election and the U.S. Senate office: James Talarico (Democratic), Ken Paxton (Republican), and Ted Brown (Libertarian), each with status "Candidate in the General Election." Jade Simmons is listed twice, as an independent, status REJECTED, and as a write-in, accepted; three other write-in candidates are Avery Ayers, Bradford "Brad" Hall, and Zul Mohamed. The state's 2026 dates page and the FEC both name Tuesday, November 3, 2026, as election day, and the state's election-law calendar set the deadline to certify party nominees to county election officers at August 28, 2026.<Cite s="sos-goelect-candidates" passage="STATUS: REJECTED" /><Cite s="sos-independent-declarations-2026" passage="Jade Simmons" /><Cite s="sos-dates-2026" passage="General election day." /><Cite s="fec-november-2026-election-date" passage="Tuesday, November 3, 2026" /><Cite s="sos-advisory-2026-21" passage="names of party nominees for statewide and district offices" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-maga-inc-august-monthly" kind="official" detail>
          <p>MAGA Inc.'s own books: the August monthly report covers July 1 through July 31, 2026, cash on hand {millions(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)}, receipts {millions(magaCash.receiptsPeriod)}, disbursements about $132,000; the July payee list includes Pfeiffer Public Affairs at $7,500 and Trump National Golf Club Washington DC at about $24,100, and no Del Ray Media line. The Texas notice is the committee's first 2026 notice for the regular November general election; its other 2026 notices were a March 11 filing for a Georgia 14th District special election, about $17,900 in text messages supporting Clay Fuller, and the August 24 South Carolina runoff notice for Darline Graham, about $828,000 year to date. Del Ray Media LLC is not new to the committee: the bulk file shows eight MAGA INC. rows to Del Ray in November 2025 for a Tennessee House special election, {millions(delRay.magaTennesseeNov2025)} in all, and four Congressional Leadership Fund rows to Del Ray dated August 31, 2026, totaling {millions(delRay.clfAug31)}.<Cite s="fec-maga-inc-august-monthly" passage="PFEIFFER PUBLIC AFFAIRS" /><Cite s="fec-form24-1952643-csv" passage='"S2026","Special-General"' /><Cite s="fec-form24-2009583" passage="Calendar YTD Per Election for Office Sought = $827711.11" /><Cite s="fec-ie-2026-bulk" passage="PLACED MEDIA: TV AND CONNECTED TV" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the ratios were counted</summary>
          <p>Talarico receipts ${usd(talarico.receipts)} divided by Paxton receipts ${usd(paxton.receipts)} is {computed.receiptsRatio}. Unitemized Talarico ${usd(talarico.unitemized)} divided by individual ${usd(talarico.individual)} is {computed.talaricoUnitemizedPct} percent. Unitemized Paxton ${usd(paxton.unitemized)} divided by individual ${usd(paxton.individual)} is {computed.paxtonUnitemizedPct} percent. MAGA Inc. ${usd(filing.total)} divided by July 31 cash ${usd(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)} is {computed.shareOfMagaCashPct} percent. Ten million divided by Thune's eight million a week is {outsideMoney.weeksAtThuneRate} weeks. Days from September 5 to November 3 are {computed.daysDisseminationToElection}. Poll margins: plus or minus {poll.moeSample} points before weighting, {poll.moeWeighted} after; Paxton job rating statewide {poll.approveStrong} strongly plus {poll.approveSomewhat} somewhat approve, {poll.disapproveSomewhat} somewhat plus {poll.disapproveStrong} strongly disapprove.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Viewers, donors, and the calendar</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Connected TV is television watched through an internet app rather than a broadcast or cable signal. The filing says the ads run there and online; it does not say in which markets or to whom. If you saw one of these spots, MAGA Inc. paid for it, not the Paxton campaign, and the words you heard are on this page.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="PLACED MEDIA: CONNECTED TV & DIGITAL ADVERTISING" /></p>

        <ol class="dated-list" data-layer="fact">
          <li><strong>September 30:</strong> the candidates' books close for their next quarterly reports to the FEC, due October 15. That is the first record of money raised after this buy.<Cite s="fec-2026-quarterly-filers" passage="10/15/2026" /></li>
          <li><strong>October 14:</strong> books close for the pre-election report, due October 22.<Cite s="fec-2026-quarterly-filers" passage="10/15/2026" /></li>
          <li><strong>October 19:</strong> early voting in person begins.<Cite s="sos-dates-2026" passage="First day of early voting by personal appearance for November 3 general election." /></li>
          <li><strong>November 3:</strong> election day, {computed.daysDisseminationToElection} days after the ads went public.<Cite s="sos-dates-2026" passage="General election day." /></li>
        </ol>

        <p data-layer="narrative">People had already paid into this race through the campaigns and through other independent-expenditure committees. More than half of Talarico's individual money, {computed.talaricoUnitemizedPct} percent, came in unitemized gifts: the committee did not have to list those donors on the report because the gifts did not exceed $200 in the year. For Paxton that share is {computed.paxtonUnitemizedPct} percent. Unitemized does not mean the committee lacks the name.<Cite s="fec-talarico-july-quarterly" passage="$36,717,034.10" /><Cite s="fec-paxton-july-quarterly" passage="$1,764,118.03" /><Cite s="fec-recording-receipts-help" passage="For each contribution that exceeds $200" /><Cite s="fec-ie-2026-bulk" passage="LONE STAR LIBERTY PAC" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Who pays MAGA Inc.</strong><span>Largest Schedule A line 17 receipts on MAGA Inc. (C00892471) Form 3X filings covering July 2025 through July 2026. Rounded in the table; cents in the proof.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={magaDonors.columns} rows={magaDonors.rows} highlightColumn={1} />
          <p class="chart-source">Committee cash is not traceable to one buy. These are the largest receipts on the nine reports; they fund the committee's account, not a labeled Texas invoice.<Cite s="fec-maga-inc-f3x-1930418" passage="BROCKMAN" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Summed from SA17 rows in files 1930418, 1943509, 1949722, 1955690, 1970306, 1978142, 1986089, 2000587, and 2009096. Miriam Adelson, Diane Hendricks, Greg Brockman, and Foris DAX, Inc. ${usd(magaDonors.adelson)} each. Konstantin Sokolov ${usd(magaDonors.sokolov)}. Gemini Trust Company ${usd(magaDonors.gemini)}. Tyler Winklevoss ${usd(magaDonors.tyler)}; Cameron Winklevoss ${usd(magaDonors.cameron)}. Securing American Greatness, Inc. ${usd(magaDonors.sag)}. JP Morgan Chase Bank, N.A. ${usd(magaDonors.jpmorgan)}. RAI Services Company and a16z Capital Management LLC ${usd(magaDonors.rai)} each. GEO Reentry Services LLC ${usd(magaDonors.geo)}. This is committee C00892471, the Form 24 filer, not C00825851 (Make America Great Again Inc.).</p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Checked against the notice</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Nine publications are in the records. They rest on fewer dispatches: the Associated Press, CNBC, and PBS NewsHour are one wire, and PBS carries "Josh Boak, Associated Press." Four called independent spending a gift to the campaign.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="DEL RAY MEDIA LLC" /><Cite s="pbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Josh Boak, Associated Press" /></p>

        <div data-layer="fact">
          <OutletCheck outlet="CBS News" claim={`wrote that Trump was &ldquo;donating $10 million&rdquo; and that &ldquo;both donations&rdquo; went to &ldquo;Del Ray Media Inc.&rdquo;`} verdict="The $5 million split and the Saturday filing check out. The spending is independent, not a donation; the vendor is Del Ray Media LLC. The same story called an $827,000 South Carolina notice a donation to Sen. Darline Graham; that too is independent spending." chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="donating $10 million" /><Cite s="fec-form24-2009583" passage="Calendar YTD Per Election for Office Sought = $827711.11" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="The Hill" claim={`wrote that MAGA Inc. &ldquo;gave $10 million to the Texas attorney general's Senate campaign, per Federal Election Commission (FEC) filings.&rdquo;`} verdict="The filings exist. A gift to the campaign is the wrong legal category." chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="thehill-hinojosa-texas-senate" passage="gave $10 million to the Texas attorney general" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="USA Today" claim={`wrote that MAGA Inc. &ldquo;has donated $10 million to the campaign&rdquo; of Paxton.`} verdict="The notice is independent spending paid to Del Ray Media LLC, not a donation to the campaign committee." chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="usatoday-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="has donated $10 million to the campaign" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="Reuters" claim={`headlined that MAGA Inc. &ldquo;gives $10 million to&rdquo; &ldquo;Paxton's Senate campaign.&rdquo;`} verdict="The $10 million figure matches the notice. A gift to the campaign is the wrong legal category. The saved page is the Reuters dispatch as carried by Yahoo." chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="reuters-via-yahoo-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="gives $10 million to" /><Cite s="reuters-via-yahoo-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="Paxton's Senate campaign" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="Associated Press" claim={`wrote that the group is spending $10 million on television and digital advertisements &ldquo;according to a Saturday filing with the Federal Election Commission.&rdquo; CNBC and PBS NewsHour ran the same dispatch.`} verdict="Checks out. Connected TV is television, so the wording matches the purpose line." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Saturday filing with the Federal Election Commission" /><Cite s="cnbc-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Saturday filing with the Federal Election Commission" /><Cite s="pbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Saturday filing with the Federal Election Commission" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="NBC News" claim={`wrote that MAGA Inc. &ldquo;had more than $400 million in the bank as of July 31.&rdquo;`} verdict={`Checks out: the August monthly close is ${millions(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)}.`} chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="nbc-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="MAGA Inc. had more than $400 million in the bank as of July 31" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="Fox News" claim={`attributed the $10 million to an FEC filing and put the candidates' fundraising at $${tracker.talaricoSince2025.toFixed(1)} million and $${tracker.paxtonSince2025} million, citing the Texas Tribune.`} verdict="Checks out. The figures are the Tribune's July 20 tracker, cumulative since January 2025, which is why they differ from the FEC pages' shorter windows." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="fox-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="according to the Texas Tribune" /><Cite s="tribune-tracker-2026-07-20" passage="raising a cumulative $72.0 million compared to $16.8 million" /></OutletCheck>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The filing, then the ads</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2>

        <div data-layer="fact">
          <CheckedBlock label="The coverage's claim: MAGA Inc. gave $10 million to Paxton's campaign" variant="contested">
            <p>Wrong. The notice is Form 24, a report of independent expenditures. The payee on both lines is Del Ray Media LLC, a media vendor, not Ken Paxton for Senate. Gantt certified the spending was not made in cooperation with a candidate or authorized committee. The $10 million itself is right: the two $5 million lines add to it, and the figure is the filing, not an ad-tracking estimate.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="DEL RAY MEDIA LLC" /><Cite s="fec-form24-certification" passage="not made in cooperation, consultation, or concert" /><Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="Total Expenditures This Period = $10000000" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
        </div>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The ads make six claims a viewer can check. Both spots put source slates on screen. The tax spot cites Punchbowl News, the Tax Foundation, Thomson Reuters, the Texas House Journal of May 25, 2019, and the Texas Tribune of June 12, 2019. The health spot cites Talarico for Texas, Navigant, and the Hoover Institution, and for the $4 trillion line cites Punchbowl and a Tax Foundation page of June 6, 2025.<Cite s="magainc-ad-talarico-frame-024" passage="TAX FOUNDATION, 7/4/25" /><Cite s="magainc-ad-paxton-frame-007" passage="TAX FOUNDATION, 6/6/25" /><Cite s="magainc-ad-paxton-frame-012" passage="COULD CLOSE 30 HOSPITALS" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Talarico's recorded property-tax votes</strong><span>House tallies as published. The 2019 row is the Tribune's April 30 count; the later rows are the House Journal.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={propertyVotes.columns} rows={propertyVotes.rows} highlightColumn={3} />
          <p class="chart-source">The ads' "voted to make it easier to increase property taxes" line matches 2019. It is selective as an account of the later record.<Cite s="magainc-ad-paxton-2026-09-05" passage="voted to make it easier to increase property taxes" /><Cite s="tribune-2019-house-vote-sb2" passage="107-40 vote" /><Cite s="house-journal-882-day06-2023" passage="SBi2 was passed by (Record 31): 133 Yeas, 4 Nays" /><Cite s="house-journal-89r-day69" passage="SBi4 was passed by (Record 3116): 143 Yeas, 0 Nays" /></p>
        </figure>

        <div data-layer="fact">
          <CheckedBlock label="Ad claim: Talarico “voted to make it easier to increase property taxes”">
            <p>Accurate as a description of two 2019 votes, selective as an account of his record. The Texas Tribune's roll call of the April 30, 2019 House vote on SB 2 is 107-40; Talarico is listed N. The House Journal for May 25, 2019, the conference vote the ads cite on screen, is 88 yeas and 50 nays; Talarico is in the nays. He is in the yeas on SB 2 of 2023 (Record 31: 133 yeas, 4 nays) and on SB 4 of 2025 (Record 3116: 143 yeas, 0 nays). Governor Abbott called the 2023 bill "the largest property tax cut in Texas history," an $18 billion package. That is the vote the Ennis statement names.<Cite s="tribune-2019-house-vote-sb2" passage="James Talarico --> N" /><Cite s="hjrnl-86r-day74-sb2" passage="(Record 1939): 88 Yeas, 50 Nays" /><Cite s="house-journal-882-day06-2023" passage="SBi2 was passed by (Record 31): 133 Yeas, 4 Nays" /><Cite s="abbott-property-tax-cut-2023" passage="the largest property tax cut in Texas history" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
          <CheckedBlock label="Ad claim: Talarico “supported a $4 trillion tax hike” (one spot) and “a trillion dollar tax hike” (the other)" variant="contested">
            <p>The two spots disagree, and the slates they cite do not name a Talarico bill with either figure. The health spot writes "$4,000,000,000,000" and cites Punchbowl News of September 9, 2025 and the Tax Foundation of June 6, 2025. The June 6 Tax Foundation page says the House-passed bill's tax provisions "would reduce federal tax revenue by $4 trillion between 2025 and 2034," a national score, not a Talarico proposal. The tax spot cites a Tax Foundation page of July 4, 2025 that says extending the 2017 tax law "would decrease federal tax revenue by $4.5 trillion from 2025 through 2034," again national revenue. The ad cites a Punchbowl News item of September 9, 2025 that we could not retrieve (the saved page is a membership wall) and a Thomson Reuters item of November 3, 2025 that we could not retrieve. The House Journal of May 25, 2019 and the Tribune of June 12, 2019 are the SB 2 vote and signing, not a trillion-dollar hike.<Cite s="magainc-ad-paxton-frame-007" passage="$4,000,000,000,000" /><Cite s="taxfoundation-house-obbba-2025-06-06" passage="reduce federal tax revenue by $4 trillion between 2025 and 2034" /><Cite s="taxfoundation-tcja-2025-07-04" passage="would decrease federal tax revenue by $4.5 trillion from 2025 through 2034" /><Cite s="tribune-abbott-sb2-2019-06-12" passage="Gov. Greg Abbott signs bill designed to limit property tax growth" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
          <CheckedBlock label="Ad claim: Talarico “supports a radical health care plan that could close rural hospitals”" variant="contested">
            <p>The ad cites Talarico for Texas (accessed March 12, 2026), Navigant of August 2019, and the Hoover Institution of October 20, 2020. His health page proposes to "join Medicare: Provide an affordable not-for-profit health care option for every American." The American Hospital Association summary of the Navigant analysis states that a Medicare-rate public option "could place as many as 55% of rural hospitals, or 1,037 hospitals across 46 states, at high risk of closure." That is a national scenario under Medicare rates, not a count of 30 Texas hospitals. The Hoover Institution paper of October 20, 2020 that the slate names is a document we could not retrieve.<Cite s="talarico-issue-health" passage="to join Medicare: Provide an affordable not-for-profit health care option for every American" /><Cite s="aha-navigant-rural-2019-08-07" passage="could place as many as 55% of rural hospitals, or 1,037 hospitals across 46 states, at high risk of closure" /><Cite s="magainc-ad-paxton-frame-012" passage="COULD CLOSE 30 HOSPITALS" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
          <CheckedBlock label="Ad claim: Talarico supported “higher taxes on social security”">
            <p>Supported in the narrow sense, misleading if read as a tax on benefits. His tax page says "eliminating the tax cap on those earning over $400,000 a year" so that "the wealthiest Americans pay their fair share into Social Security." That is a payroll tax on earnings above $400,000, not a tax on Social Security checks. The on-screen slate for this line is the same Punchbowl / Tax Foundation / Thomson Reuters / House Journal / Tribune cluster as the tax-hike line.<Cite s="talarico-issue-taxes" passage="eliminating the tax cap on those earning over $400,000 a year" /><Cite s="magainc-ad-talarico-frame-021" passage="ON SOCIAL SECURITY" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
          <CheckedBlock label="Ad claim: Paxton's plan doubles the child tax credit, adds medical write-offs, and a $50,000 deduction for first-time homes">
            <p>Published. In a July 31 video Paxton says, "That's why I'm unveiling my protecting Texas Promise Agenda," then names a "$25,000 tax deduction" for out-of-pocket medical expenses, a "$50,000 home down payment deduction," and "It doubles the child tax credit." The Texas Tribune the same day reported the $25,000 medical deduction, the $50,000 first-home deduction, and a proposed $4,400 child credit. CBS Texas on August 4 covered the plan. The records we hold do not state a cost, an offset, or whether the deductions are above the line.<Cite s="paxton-texas-promise-video-2026-08" passage="That's why I'm unveiling my protecting Texas Promise Agenda" /><Cite s="tribune-paxton-plan-2026-07-31" passage="a $25,000 deduction for any out-of-pocket medical expense" /><Cite s="cbstexas-paxton-plan-allen-2026-08" passage="annual $25,000 deduction on federal taxes for all out-of-pocket medical expenses" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
        </div>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how this was added and checked</summary>
          <p>Independent-expenditure line opposing Talarico: ${usd(filing.opposeTalarico)}. Line supporting Paxton: ${usd(filing.supportPaxton)}. 5,000,000 + 5,000,000 = 10,000,000; the notice's own total is ${usd(filing.total)}. The spots were downloaded from the MAGA War Room posts with yt-dlp on September 8 (30 seconds each), transcribed with local speech recognition (FluidAudio Parakeet, via undertone), and read frame by frame (one frame a second). SB 2 votes are read from the 2019 House Journal and the Tribune roll call; 2023 and 2025 votes from the House Journal PDFs. Campaign pages were saved as served on September 8. Slate sources were fetched once each with a browser impersonation, then the Internet Archive; Punchbowl, Thomson Reuters of November 3, 2025, and the Hoover paper of October 20, 2020 did not yield a usable page.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">After Saturday</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 6 The Hill reported Gina Hinojosa, the Democratic nominee for governor, telling CNN's Dana Bash she welcomed the attention on Texas after the buy. Talarico's own post that Saturday, "This is not capitalism; this is crony capitalism," does not name the ads, MAGA Inc., Paxton, or Trump; the campaign's response on the record is the Ennis statement.<Cite s="thehill-hinojosa-texas-senate" passage="I welcome the attention on Texas" /><Cite s="talarico-x-2026-09-05" passage="This is not capitalism; this is crony capitalism." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 8 the Talarico campaign posted a one-minute YouTube ad. In it David Maxwell, a former Paxton aide, says "My name is David Maxwell. Ken Paxton ended my five decade career because I blew the whistle on his corruption." A New York Times story on the ad the same day did not let its page be saved, and no other coverage capture landed in one attempt.<Cite s="talarico-maxwell-ad-2026-09-08" passage="My name is David Maxwell. Ken Paxton ended my five decade career because I blew the whistle on his corruption." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Watching: MAGA Inc.'s next 24-hour or 48-hour notices, the October 15 quarterly reports, the October 22 pre-election reports, and early voting from October 19.<Cite s="fec-2026-quarterly-filers" passage="10/15/2026" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <ul data-layer="fact">
